  <li><strong>Web page:</strong> <a href="https://protesilaos.com/emacs/denote#h:175b3b5f-4490-4cb0-81d7-26652cf08e79">https://protesilaos.com/emacs/denote#h:175b3b5f-4490-4cb0-81d7-26652cf08e79</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Info node:</strong> Evaluate <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">(info "(denote) Abstractions for a cache or database")</code>.</li>
</ul>

<p>I did this in response to issue 724 by kimsaram32, with participation
from Aaron Jensen, relict007, Samuel W. Flint, mentalisttraceur,
Mirko Hernandez: <a href="https://github.com/protesilaos/denote/issues/724">https://github.com/protesilaos/denote/issues/724</a>.</p>

<h3>Disable the “fancy” file prompt</h3>

<p>By default, Denote’s file prompt reformats file names. A Denote-style
file name is split into columns so that its components are easier to
read. Users who prefer to see the actual file names can now set the
user option <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">denote-fancy-file-prompt</code> to a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">nil</code> value.</p>

<p>I did this in response to issue 732 by Honza Pokorný:
<a href="https://github.com/protesilaos/denote/issues/732">https://github.com/protesilaos/denote/issues/732</a>.</p>

<p>Note that in addition to this user option advanced users can also
change the relevant completion metadata. Check the variable
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">denote-file-prompt-extra-metadata</code> and the functions mentioned
therein.</p>

<h3>Extra backslashes in query links now result in an error</h3>

<p>Denote supports direct links to files as well as “query links”. Those
look like links but actually produce a buffer with files that match
the given query for file contents or file names. Query links are
documented at length in the manual. They are the functional equivalent
of the commands <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">denote-dired</code> (search for file names and put them in
a Dired buffer) and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">denote-grep</code> (search for file contents and put
them in an Xref buffer).</p>

<p>Queries that have extra backslashes will now report an error. This can
happen when users try their regular expression in something like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-x
re-builder</code> and then bring it into the query link. That is a common
mistake with Emacs regular expressions in general (I do it as well):
in Lisp programs we write double backslashes but in interactive calls
we use single backslashes.</p>

<p>I did this in response to issue 733 by hapst3r: <a href="https://github.com/protesilaos/denote/issues/733">https://github.com/protesilaos/denote/issues/733</a>.
I actually did it as part of a live stream, which you can watch if you are
curious about the technicalities: <a href="https://protesilaos.com/codelog/2026-10-30-emacs-live-denote-refinements/">https://protesilaos.com/codelog/2026-10-30-emacs-live-denote-refinements/</a>.</p>

